WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.290
We have to be stewards of our
environment, we have to be able

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.980
to protect the rich biodiversity
that's on our planet because we

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.600
benefit from that. Scientific
research is critical because

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.140
that gives us the information we
need to really understand what

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.390
it is we need to put our
resources into protecting. My

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.810
name is Tracy Seimon. I'm the
director of the Wildlife

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.980
Conservation Society's molecular
lab based at the Bronx Zoo.  We

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.640
do a lot of diagnostic testing
to screen animals that are in

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.380
our collection. We do PCR to
monitor for a lot of different

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.280
pathogens. We also help
develop portable technologies

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.640
that we can take into the field
and really empower our

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.940
researchers around the world to
be able to do diagnostic testing

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.370
on the spot. My career
trajectory has taken a very

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.670
circuitous path. Because I was
really interested in studying

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.790
disease and mechanisms of
disease, and particularly at the

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.280
time I really thought I was
gonna go into cancer research.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:08.010
On the side I started studying
amphibians. And this was because

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.450
I had met Anton Seimon. He's a
climate scientist, who's now my

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.500
husband. And he was leading
expeditions down to Peru to

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.590
understand how climate change is
impacting high mountain

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.070
environments. And so on one of
their expeditions, they were

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.650
speaking to the local
communities, the Quechua

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.940
communities, who informed them
that they had been seeing a lot

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.600
of frog die offs. Anton knew I
worked in a experimental

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.960
pathology program and reached
out to me. And so that's how I

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.730
came aboard the project. I love
high mountain environments. This

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.000
is an area that I've invested
research in over two decades

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.850
now. We've been doing continuous
monitoring of amphibians up in

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.080
the upper reaches of the Andean
ecosystem. This is probably the

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:01.620
highest herpetological site up
here. It's at 17,000 feet. What we're

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.280
collecting is Pleurodema
marmoratum. Amphibians they're

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.670
basically indicator species that
tell us a lot about how the

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.850
environment is changing. What
we're doing is is swabbing these

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:20.490
frogs to test for amphibian chytrid fungus. 
We first found amphibian chytrid fungus here in 2003. 

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.760
So as the glaciers are receding, you're
getting new ponds that are

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.510
forming higher and higher up.
When these new ponds form that

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.650
creates the environment to where
pollens get deposited up there,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.920
and microbes, And that starts
building the soils. And then

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.310
those soils can start allowing
growth of plants, which then

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.640
bring up insects. And so the
whole biosphere is actually

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.910
rising in response to the rising
global temperatures. 

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:52.320
We also have research part of the lab
where we take on projects with

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.620
collaborators. Some of that
diagnostic support has been

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.180
through developing eDNA tests.
eDNA stands for environmental

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.200
DNA. And it is DNA that's shed
from organisms that exist in an

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.830
environment. Its DNA that can be
captured through, for example,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.240
water samples, soil samples, and
even air samples. 

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.590
And it's a powerful tool that allows
conservationists to search for

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.950
rare and threatened species, and
look for evidence of specific

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.340
species, you can use it to
perform biodiversity assessments

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:29.130
to look at all the organisms in
the environment. 

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:32.130
Our first area where we've opened the door to
eDNA analysis for biodiversity

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.910
assessment was actually done on
Mount Everest in 2019. So the

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.510
expedition actually was very
interdisciplinary, pulled

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.690
scientists from all all over the
world. And so they needed some

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.650
biologists. So our idea was to
do basically collect water

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.680
samples along an elevational
gradient up to the highest

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.700
ponds. When we were camping on
on the glacier at Everest base

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:59.520
camp, we were sleeping on a
moving glacier. And the sound of

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.400
the crackling of the glacier
beneath your tent as you're

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.560
falling asleep and the
thunderous roars of the

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.810
avalanche is coming down. It's
both scary, but at the same

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:17.070
time, it's, it's just amazing to
hear it. 

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:21.330
From these 10 sites that we identified, we collected
over 20 liters of water. When we

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:26.760
brought that back to the lab, we
performed analysis of the eDNA

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.400
that we captured from the
filters from those 20 liters of

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.260
water. And we performed whole
genome shotgun sequencing, so we

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.410
sequenced everything. But we
also performed DNA

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.280
meta barcoding, where we targeted
what you call a DNA barcoding

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.690
gene, which is a mitochondrial
DNA target, to then identify

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.380
organisms, often down to the
species level. Out of this

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:56.550
analysis we identified 187
taxonomic orders from 36 phylum,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.030
which is quite impressive. It's
across the entire tree of life.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.960
So we identified everything from
a few viruses, bacteria, fungi,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.520
plants, animals. When you put
into context, the number of

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.430
taxonomic orders that we
recovered from these 20 liters

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.230
of water. When we compare them
to total known taxonomic orders

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.430
of around the world, we
discovered basically 1/6 of

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.260
global taxonomic orders just on
this one mountain above 4500

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.350
meters. Which is quite
remarkable, especially when you

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:33.780
think about areas around the
earth above 4500 meters occupies

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.890
only about 3% of landmass. It
was quite striking that we found

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:46.380
that that level of diversity up
on the high slopes of Everest. 

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.680
I mean, we're really pushing the
boundaries with trying to detect

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.330
eDNA. And one of our projects,
where we're focused on trying to

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:58.080
detect this very rare turtle
species in Vietnam called

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.170
Rafetus swinhoei which is the
Yangtze giant softshell turtle.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.970
Our researchers can go out and
systematically test lakes and

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.300
rivers for the presence of this
species. And it's really

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.860
critical that we find additional
individuals in order to save the

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.710
species from going extinct
because they're they're really

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:21.780
on the verge of blinking out. As
stewards of the earth we need to

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.530
be able to ensure that
generations to come  benefit

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.510
from the rich biodiversity on
our planet.

